-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
How to generate the full third-party list
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Run from a clean checkout; write the outputs to a build folder, not the tree.

Elixir (Hex):
  mix deps                      # list; licences are in each deps/<pkg>/hex_metadata.config
  # or the hex_metadata "licenses" field per package:
  for f in deps/*/hex_metadata.config; do echo "$f"; grep -A2 licenses "$f"; done
  # alternative scanner: the `licensir` Mix task (a dev-only dependency, new
  # dependency needs the owner's OK)

npm (web/), full transitive tree:
  cd web && npm ci
  npx license-checker-rseidelsohn --production --summary
  npx license-checker-rseidelsohn --production --json --out <build>/npm.json
  # fail list: --failOn "GPL;AGPL;SSPL;BUSL" (match the policy below)

pub (apps/mobile/):
  cd apps/mobile && flutter pub get
  flutter pub deps --style=list             # package list
  # licences: read LICENSE in each package of the pub cache; no field to query.
  # A scanner such as `flutter_oss_licenses` can emit them (dev tool, needs approval).

Containers / base images:
  docker image inspect and the upstream licence pages; attribution only.

Also scan the history and the tree for secrets (gitleaks, trufflehog) - a
different job, listed in T-1521 section 5, item 2.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CI check proposal (not implemented here)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
A new job "licence scan" in .github/workflows/ci.yml, path-filtered to
mix.lock, web/package-lock.json, apps/mobile/pubspec.lock:
  1. Run the three generators above; write one report per ecosystem as a CI
     artifact (the per-release licence report of T-1521 section 5, item 4).
  2. Fail on a licence in the deny list: GPL, AGPL, SSPL, BUSL (proposed rule,
     to be confirmed by the owner and a lawyer). LGPL, MPL, EPL: allowed only
     if listed in an allow file with a one-line reason.
  3. Fail on a package with no licence information, unless allow-listed.
  4. Compare the generated THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt with the committed one at
     release time; a difference fails the release job.
The check needs a lint-sized task of its own and, for the pub part, a tool
decision; it must not weaken any existing gate.
